 small hill that is the  final hero of the coaster which we very  often see on these things and that  should more or less finish it this is  just going to make sure that once it  gets it over this hill it's already  going to break a little bit on the top  of this and not just barrage into the  final brake run and a ridiculous speed  so let's see how this is going to work  all right now the pieces are finally  fitting here and just in time for the  coaster to make its run so I'm just  gonna quickly look at it off right and  then give you an on-ride of the coaster  now I have to say this is really a  general idea of what the unlocker  courses are like it's a bit different  from the standard model it's actually  very different from the standard model  but this is usually also what they do  look like with that that very dominating  sort of lift here that's quite steep and  the crazy amount of supports and support  structures right in the middle of the  track and the track is kind of  interweaving and ducking over and  underneath its own track and finally  coming back with that very small closer  to ground section and hopping into the  brake runs just like that it does break  a little bit much with those trims  though that's something I might want to  look at same goes for the trims at the  top but then again those trims and block  breaks can also help you get the pacing  of the track right without having to  actually edit the track itself but I'll  definitely slow down these strings at  the end a little bit  they shouldn't be really slowing down  the coaster to the speed that we  have it roll into the station with so  the time has come to write this crazy  thing and something I do in a note is  that these things very often seem  unsmooth on POV camera because of the  way that the camera moves around in  planet coaster to anticipate curves just  like they're on the drop so it can seem  very unsmooth even if parts of the track  are some weapon smooth just by how small  the track moves around it is quite hard  to make these seem like a very smooth  track in the game but anyway the speed  over here is just about kind of what  we're looking for a little bit slow  maybe in parts like this but it usually  does have layout which is very slow on  those top parts where does the weird  things like having the hang time in the  diving loop or having the outside banked  curves and having those dips in the  middle of the track where it goes to the  ground and make some speed and yeah  that's basically the very standard kind  of SNS el loco kind of layouts a couple  of interesting trivia now that I'm busy  with this anyway I have this is really  just about what you want to do for these  courses and there are different ways  that you can just you know use your  creativity on these things because they  have such weird elements but this kind  of fits into my earlier arrow kind of  episodes which I had before because as  an S which is the company that makes  these things which I just accidentally  named dropped but it's actually a  company that bought the rights to all of  the assets I believe all of them from  arrow after they went bankrupt so it's  kind of the legacy of arrow as well and  they have also built the 40 courses that  arrow has built as well that's probably  material for a later episode but it's  quite interesting and also there is  actually a 7th el loco goes through  which not that many people know about  and it's analog which I believe was only  open for a couple of hours  it was built in Tokyo and it was  terrible it was actually built by the  Japanese branch of the company or  actually a company in which bought the  entire as an S Company  and it didn't turn out all that well  even if it had some that classical  a local kind of traits to it so these  things as weird and small and quirky as  they may be are actually some very  well-respected coasters with some very  interesting elements and provide a very  thrilling experience on a 